hi i'm nala from quicktake in  minneapolis right now police traffic  stops have declined significantly  since the death of george floyd i'm  joined by sarah holder from bloomberg  city lab  who's reported on the story and is here  to share more on the data  and her reporting hi sarah thank you so  much for joining us today  hey great to be here awesome so sarah  can you start us  off and just tell us what exactly you  found from your reporting  yeah so along with my colleagues rachel  doddle and marie patino who are great  data journalists  we looked through uh the rate of traffic  stops in minneapolis  um traffic stops are one of the most  frequent police community  interactions nationwide the stanford  university's open policing project  estimates that about 50 000 drivers and  pedestrians are stopped each day  um for these routine stops for moving  violations like speeding tickets  um or equipment violations like a broken  taillight  um or sometimes pre-tech stops which are  stops that police make  um that are sort of intending to find  evidence of  more serious crimes um in minneapolis  uh these stops are quite frequent as i  mentioned  as they are in many other american  cities  um but since may 25th when george floyd  was killed by a police officer they're  down about 80 percent  that is quite a significant number and  you mentioned that  you know there seems to be this trend of  police disengaging disengaging after  mass protests against racial injustice  are there any other examples of this  happening across the country  do we see people or police kind of  pulling back  um their efforts or their work or the  the  are declining um when when we do have  mass protests or we  do come into incidents similar to the  death of george void  yeah i mean so it's hard to you know  draw a direct correlation between these  two events but we did see you know a  small  dip in traffic stops during coronavirus  um where fewer cars were on the road  but it was really nothing compared to  the 80 drop  as as we talked about after the killing  of george floyd and the protests across  the city  you know i wasn't able to talk to police  officers in minneapolis and they helped  with some of the data research but we  couldn't you know sit down and talk  through why um this is happening but we  are seeing you know anecdotal evidence  in minneapolis of  9-1-1 calls taking a long time to be  responded to  um police officers leaving the force  retiring early  taking medical leave um and that's  something we have seen in other american  cities  uh as well um perhaps it is you know  due to increased scrutiny over activity  perhaps it's due to shifting priorities  um perhaps it's due to being short  staffed um  and distracted um but you know more  research is really needed on  what traffic stops are still being um  conducted  uh you know even though it's an 80 drop  we saw that some of the racial  disparities in who is being stopped  have continued um so while the volume is  down some of the issues that  activists and criminal justice advocates  have highlighted um in in these  you know over policing of black and  brown communities have not appeared to  have changed us significantly and you  mentioned specifically traffic stops  what else is the data telling us  specifically  is there a historical context that we  should be aware of is our traffic stops  typically an area where police do make a  lot of arrests  yeah so i mean in minneapolis the  hennepin county public defender's office  has done a lot of work on this year over  year they find that  though minneapolis has more of a larger  white population than a black population  black drivers are stopped more  frequently than wet drivers  again year after year um and you know  these racial disparities have narrowed a  bit  as the as the overall volume of stops  have decreased um but those still appear  and you know there are several  consequences to being  stopped by police whether that's a fine  or a fee um  an arrest um you know a violent  altercation as in the case of philando  castile  um these can also be traumatizing for  people again if you're  you're stopped for no reason or for a  reason that you don't understand um  and it as the aclu says it can erode  community trust  um but at the same time you know some  traffic stops are necessary to  help curb dangerous driving um and so  it's really  i think uh a growing balance um  is is being desired from from folks on  the ground absolutely  and were there specific reasons that you  came across that people were typically  being stopped for or was it just more  general yeah so i think that's a place  where  data more data research needs to be done  but as i  as i said i guess it's mostly moving  violations  equipment violations you know suspicious  vehicles  all those might be reasons why police  might pull someone over  or those sorts of pretext stops there is  a program in minneapolis called lights  on  which was instituted after the killing  of philando castile where  if you are pulled over for a broken  taillight for example instead of getting  a ticket you can get a voucher  um to repair that violation and  hopefully stop you from getting pulled  over again  um again there can be racial disparities  in how those vouchers are  handed out um so it's not you know a  perfect solution  uh says um you know uh  advocates from the aclu um but that is  one um  one change that we've seen in the past  four years um and could  you know change the kinds of traffic  stops that we're seeing out of  minneapolis  in the years to come absolutely and  you've mentioned philando castile a  couple of times now um i just wanted to  touch on that briefly  and talk about some of the consequences  that we do see when people are stopped  by police  one that is top of mind i'm sure for all  of us right now is brianna taylor  can you speak to that a little bit what  exactly or did your research find any  any sort of consequences when we do see  over policing in community  yeah absolutely i mean i think that  that's such a  salient example of a you know a police  uh  interaction gone very wrong and i think  that that's why  you know um activists who are hoping to  reimagine policing  um cut funding to policing and invest it  in other social services have identified  traffic stops as just one area  where um police could be replaced uh in  you know these  functions by other unarmed enforcement  officers so you know in berkeley  california they voted to create a new  department of transportation  um where unarmed officers would handle  police traffic stops again you know  stopping  dangerous driving but they would never  have a gun when they are  stopping someone for speeding um and so  i think it comes from  a fear that these interactions  can lead to harmful outcomes for  communities of color and  and um people like philando castile  absolutely and with all of this  disengagement we are seeing has that had  any impact on  the conversations we've we've seen  occurring this summer  in terms of you know defunding the  police or abolishing the police  yeah i mean i think it's uh it's too  soon to tell  about a lot of the momentum that's  happening right now i know that a lot of  cities have had their budget  conversations already and have made  small cuts or small increases um and so  i think in the budget  discussions to follow there might be  more of an emphasis  on where can we invest in in you know  the department of transportation in safe  driving um  and where can we invest in in the police  and yeah i mean i think in minneapolis  especially that's a really  salient conversation because it's where  um  these recent rash of protests have  started  um because it was where george ford was  killed and so i think that they're sort  of at the forefront of these  conversations and  we'll definitely be looking at  their police department as an example of  you know how to how to do things better  absolutely absolutely and as you  mentioned this has been definitely a hot  button topic  all summer long and i'm sure we're going  to see it carry on um into  well into the rest of the year so that  leads me to my next question  in terms of police disengaging have you  seen any other reasons why  they are kind of pulling back on their  work or stepping back from their work  following all of these protests we've  seen over summer  yeah i mean it's again like it would be  great to be able to you know talk to  some  police officers from minneapolis  specifically about what their priorities  are  right now i think that perhaps  they have been pulled in different  directions perhaps  it is has been a hard year coronavirus  has  has changed police priorities a lot um  as i said too like there are you know  maybe  50 fewer officers in august as there  were at the beginning of the year  um so perhaps they're short-staffed um  and um  yeah i think i think that's a  conversation that has been  happening um among many minneapolis city  council members  um and i think that they you know have  been having community conversations  about  uh what the police um are doing  but i think it's it's good to note that  without more data  on again what um police traffic stops  have been continuing so you know that 80  percent drop like where is the  the brunt of the activity being focused  on so we don't know  um if that is you know focusing on these  dangerous driving incidents  or if it is um focusing on speeding or  or what um and so that would be really  helpful to know  as well and thank you so much for  bringing us back to that number that 80  drop which is such a significant  decrease  um can you speak to that number a little  bit have we ever seen a drop like this  before  uh so not uh not to my knowledge again  like  coronavirus changes everything but at  the same time  um the dip that we saw in march and  april  is just so small compared to the dip  that we saw in  in may and in june and you know  continuing through the summer  um so i mean to put that number in  context um  police were making about 351 traffic  stops a week  uh before may 25th and now they're  making about 70 a week  so um it's a it's just a really really  stark figure and and um  it'll be interesting to to learn more  about the why which i think  you know you're getting at um right now  and i would love to learn more about  that too  absolutely and that is quite a change i  think from going to almost 400 to about  70 a week that that is definitely quite  a change  um and that leads me to my next question  is there anything that  you reported on or that maybe you found  out in your research that didn't make it  into your article  yeah i mean i've been doing some  reporting on these calls to defund or to  reimagine policing in minneapolis the  city council had  you know agreed to pursue a plan to  create an office of violent prevention  and to disband the police department as  it exists right now and  to you know recreate uh and and  reimagine what  it looks like um and i think that they  sort of had the boldest plan for doing  so  um but because of some bureaucratic  issues some  um some uh technicalities with the  city's charter or their constitution  they were not able to pursue that plan  um that  that many city council members were um  backing  um so i think that you know this  disengagement or this pullback  um is coming across a backdrop of just a  lot of conversations about where  policing goes next in minneapolis and i  think  um it'll be interesting to see where  this office of violent prevention  violence prevention goes um and how  smaller reforms might trickle into  the minneapolis police department in the  months to come  absolutely absolutely and what exactly  was your intent in seeking out this  information  what was your goal when you first  started reporting on this story and  did you kind of reach that end goal or  did you find out what you were seeking  that's a that's a really good question i  think often with these data projects you  kind of go in looking for as much  information as you can get and  hope the data can you know lead you  somewhere i think we were just really  interested in traffic stops because they  are  this just very frequent  uh space of interaction that you don't  think about you know it's not a violent  crime  necessarily it's sort of just a routine  um going about your day might be stopped  by a police officer  on the side of the road and as we've  talked about it can lead to  pretty intense consequences um so we're  just interested  in examining the behavior of police in  this realm  um also because this is a space where  there have been all these proposals to  um  you know break up those duties and so i  guess we were trying to figure out  how police spend their time um and  whether anything  has changed um since the the death of  george floyd and the  protests that have happened after and we  did find that you know  uh things had changed quite a bit i  guess the next question that we would  want to  keep pulling data to answer is for how  long  whether traffic stops will bounce back  up to  pre-may 25th levels and when that will  happen  um and then as you rightly pointed out  like  what's happening in other cities um and  and is this  number being reflected nationwide and i  think that's such a good point  for you to make whether this will be the  same change or whether we'll kind of see  these numbers climb back  up to nearly 400 stops a week again or  more  um once we kind of have come out of the  the direct aftermath of most of these  protests  um so thank you so much i think this is  a great place for us to stop  thank you so much for joining us today  sarah i think this is such an important  conversation for us to continue to have  and to  engage with absolutely thank you so much  for having me this was great  awesome take care and be sure to follow  quick take  for all your news updates  you

